  "output": "Now I have everything I need for a thorough analysis. Here's the complete performance review:\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis — HR Policy Bot\n\nThis is a Python/vanilla-JS codebase (no React). The findings are grouped by category with code-level specifics.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Redundant DB Connection Opens (closest analog to N+1)\n\n**File:** `src/data/db.py` — every method\n\nEvery database operation does `async with aiosqlite.connect(...)`, which opens and closes a new SQLite connection. A single query processing cycle produces this pattern:\n\n```\nsave_query()                    → open/close\n  └─ (fetch phase, per URL)\n     get_cached(url_1)          → open/close\n     get_cached(url_2)          → open/close\n     get_cached(url_3)          → open/close\n     ...up to 32 for 8 agents × 4 URLs each\nsave_query() again              → open/close  (upsert after completion)\nsave_orchestrator_response()    → open/close\n```\n\n**Fix:** Hold a persistent connection with WAL mode enabled:\n\n```python\n# db.py\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path: Path = _DB_PATH) -> None:\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._conn: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n\n    async def initialize(self) -> None:\n        self._conn = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n        self._conn.row_factory = aiosqlite.Row\n        await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n        await self._conn.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL\")\n        await self._conn.executescript(_SCHEMA)\n        await self._conn.commit()\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._conn:\n            await self._conn.close()\n            self._conn = None\n\n    # All methods use self._conn directly instead of context-managed connect()\n    async def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n        await self._conn.execute(\"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO queries ...\", (...))\n        await self._conn.commit()\n```\n\nAdd `await db.close()` to the FastAPI shutdown event.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Global Domain Lock Sleeps While Held (High-Impact Bug)\n\n**File:** `src/fetch/engine.py:74-87`\n\n```python\nasync def _fetch_with_concurrency(self, url: str) -> str:\n    domain = self._get_domain(url)\n    async with self._domain_lock:          # ← single global lock\n        last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n        delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n        if delay_required > 0:\n            await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)  # ← sleeping INSIDE the lock\n        self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.time()\n```\n\nWhen one specialist sleeps inside `_domain_lock`, all other specialists fetching from any domain are blocked waiting for the same lock. With 8 specialists running in parallel, this can serialize all domain-rate-limit checks.\n\n**Fix:** Use per-domain locks so only same-domain fetches block each other:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, ...) -> None:\n    ...\n    self._domain_locks: dict[str, asyncio.Lock] = defaultdict(asyncio.Lock)\n    # remove self._domain_lock\n\nasync def _fetch_with_concurrency(self, url: str) -> str:\n    domain = self._get_domain(url)\n    async with self._domain_locks[domain]:       # per-domain, not global\n        last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n        delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n        if delay_required > 0:\n            await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)\n        self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.time()\n    async with self._domain_semaphores[domain]:\n        return await self._do_fetch(url)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Policy Registry Read 8 Times at Startup\n\n**File:** `src/agents/specialist.py:72-75`\n\n```python\ndef _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n    with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n        registry = json.load(f)           # ← disk read + JSON parse, called once per specialist\n    return [r for r in registry if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n`get_specialists()` creates all 8 specialists at startup, so this reads and parses the same JSON file 8 times.\n\n**Fix:**\n\n```python\n_REGISTRY: list[dict] | None = None\n\ndef _load_registry() -> list[dict]:\n    global _REGISTRY\n    if _REGISTRY is None:\n        with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n            _REGISTRY = json.load(f)\n    return _REGISTRY\n\ndef _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n    return [r for r in _load_registry() if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Regex Recompiled on Every URL Normalization\n\n**File:** `src/fetch/engine.py:67-72`\n\n```python\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    import re                              # ← fine (cached by Python)\n    if re.search(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$', url):\n        # ↑ pattern string compiled on every call\n```\n\nWith 32 URLs fetched per query, this compiles the same regex 32 times.\n\n**Fix:** Compile at module level:\n\n```python\nimport re\n_LAWS_ACT_RE = re.compile(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$')\n\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    if _LAWS_ACT_RE.search(url):\n        return url.rstrip('/') + '/FullText.html'\n    return url\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. No In-Memory Cache Layer Over SQLite\n\n**File:** `src/fetch/cache.py`, `src/data/db.py`\n\nEvery cache hit still goes to SQLite. During a single request, the same policy document URL can be checked by multiple specialists (if they share instruments). Each check opens a new DB connection (see issue 1) and runs a SQL query.\n\n**Fix:** Add an LRU in-memory layer in `FetchCache`:\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass FetchCache:\n    def __init__(self, ...) -> None:\n        ...\n        self._memory: OrderedDict[str, str] = OrderedDict()  # url → content\n        self._memory_max = 64\n\n    async def get(self, url: str) -> CacheEntry | None:\n        if url in self._memory:\n            content = self._memory[url]\n            self._memory.move_to_end(url)\n            return CacheEntry(url=url, content=content, ttl_hours=168)\n        cached = await self._db_manager.get_cached(url)\n        if cached is None:\n            return None\n        entry = CacheEntry(url=cached.url, content=cached.content_text, ...)\n        self._memory[url] = entry.content\n        if len(self._memory) > self._memory_max:\n            self._memory.popitem(last=False)\n        return entry\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. Gemini System Prompt Not Using `system_instruction`\n\n**File:** `src/llm/client.py:70-76`\n\n```python\nasync def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, ...) -> str:\n    m = self._make_model(model)\n    contents = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"parts\": [f\"{system_prompt}\\n\\n{user_message}\"]}]\n```\n\nThe system prompt is concatenated into the user message. The Gemini API has a native `system_instruction` parameter. Using it correctly separates concerns and keeps the user-turn shorter (cheaper tokens), and enables Gemini's context caching for the system prompt on paid tiers.\n\n**Fix:**\n\n```python\ndef _make_model(self, model: str | None = None, json_output: bool = False, system_instruction: str | None = None) -> genai.GenerativeModel:\n    generation_config = {\"temperature\": ..., \"top_p\": ...}\n    if json_output:\n        generation_config[\"response_mime_type\"] = \"application/json\"\n    return genai.GenerativeModel(\n        model_name=model or self._model_name,\n        generation_config=generation_config,\n        system_instruction=system_instruction or \"\",\n    )\n\nasync def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, ...) -> str:\n    m = self._make_model(model, system_instruction=system_prompt)\n    contents = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"parts\": [user_message]}]  # user message only\n    return await self._generate(m, contents)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. Latent Bug: `CacheEntry.expires_at` References Non-existent Field\n\n**File:** `src/fetch/cache.py:26-28`\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass CacheEntry:\n    url: str\n    content: str\n    ttl_hours: int\n    # fetched_at is NOT a dataclass field ↓\n    \n    @property\n    def expires_at(self) -> datetime:\n        fetched_at_str = self.fetched_at  # ← AttributeError at runtime\n```\n\n`fetched_at` is never set on the dataclass. `is_valid()` calls `expires_at`, so calling `entry.is_valid()` anywhere would raise `AttributeError`. Currently neither method is called in the live code path (expiry is handled in SQL), but it's a silent trap.\n\n**Fix:** Add the field or delete the dead code:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass CacheEntry:\n    url: str\n    content: str\n    ttl_hours: int\n    fetched_at: datetime = field(default_factory=datetime.utcnow)\n    status_code: int | None = None\n    content_length: int | None = None\n```\n\n---\n\n### 8. Frontend: DOM Query on Every Keystroke\n\n**File:** `static/js/app.js:62-65`\n\n```javascript\nqueryInput.addEventListener('input', () => {\n    const hasText = queryInput.value.trim().length > 0;\n    sendButton.disabled = !hasText || isProcessing();  // ← isProcessing() runs querySelector\n    ...\n});\n\nfunction isProcessing() {\n    return !!currentQueryId && \n           agentList.querySelector('.status-dot[data-status=\"working\"]') !== null;\n    //      ↑ DOM traversal on every keypress\n}\n```\n\n**Fix:** Track processing state with a boolean flag rather than querying the DOM:\n\n```javascript\nlet processingActive = false;\n\nfunction setProcessing(active) {\n    processingActive = active;\n    sendButton.disabled = active;\n    queryInput.disabled = active;\n}\n\nfunction isProcessing() { return processingActive; }\n```\n\nUpdate `setProcessing(true)` in `submitQuery()` and `setProcessing(false)` in `resetProcessingState()`.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Dead / Unreachable Frontend Code\n\n**File:** `static/js/app.js`\n\n- `updateMessageWithMetadata()` (lines 276-317) — defined but never called. `handleResponseComplete()` writes directly to `innerHTML` instead.\n- `handleResponseChunk()` (lines 172-183) — handles `response_chunk` messages, but the server never sends that type (only `response_complete`).\n- `wrapWithCitations()` (lines 482-492) — only called from `updateMessageWithMetadata()`, which is dead.\n\n**Recommendation:** Remove all three functions. Approximately 60 lines of dead code that the browser still parses and the engine stores in memory.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| # | File | Impact | Fix Effort |\n|---|------|--------|------------|\n| 1 | `db.py` — per-op connection open/close | High | Medium |\n| 2 | `engine.py` — global lock held during sleep | High | Low |\n| 3 | `specialist.py` — registry read 8× | Low | Trivial |\n| 4 | `engine.py` — regex recompiled per URL | Low | Trivial |\n| 5 | `cache.py` — no in-memory layer | Medium | Medium |\n| 6 | `client.py` — system_instruction API | Medium | Low |\n| 7 | `cache.py` — latent AttributeError bug | Bug | Trivial |\n| 8 | `app.js` — DOM query on every keystroke | Low | Low |\n| 9 | `app.js` — 3 dead functions | Hygiene | Trivial |\n\nIssues 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, and 9 can all be fixed in under an hour and have no risk. Issue 1 (persistent DB connection) is the most impactful and should be prioritized alongside issue 2 (domain lock bug).\n",
